 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "Put $\\mathcal A_n=\\operatorname{Mod}(B_n)$ and $F_{m,n}(P)=P\\otimes_{B_m}B_n$ on complexes. The degreewise adjunction is the enriched chain-complex adjunction: $a:P\\to\\operatorname{Res}_{m,n}Q$ corresponds to $\\widetilde a(x\\otimes b)=b\\,a(x)$ and the inverse sends $v$ to $x\\mapsto v(x\\otimes1)$. Balancing proves the inverse identities; the same formulas in each degree commute with $d(h)=d_Qh-(-1)^{|h|}h d_P$. They apply to module sheaves, by the ringed-topos module adjunction with identity underlying topos. Thus a complex in $\\mathscr S(B)$ is exactly a strict section with structure maps $F_{m,n}P_m\\to P_n$. Compatibility for adjacent arrows gives all longer arrows, using the associative tensor rebracketing; under the displayed formulas this is exactly the strict restriction-form cocycle. Morphisms are identified by the same calculation. The differential and semilinear structure commute in precisely the two descriptions.",
   "uses": [
    "def-weil-sheaf-of-modules-category",
    "def-pc-sheaf-restrict-scalars",
    "def-pc-cochain-complex-modules",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-module-adjunction"
   ],
   "elementary": true,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Equip each $\\operatorname{Ch}(\\mathcal A_n)$ with the flat model of [lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model]. Check the left Quillen hypothesis, including the nonflat case. If $i:P\\to Q$ is a cofibration, its degreewise cokernel $H$ is flat-term K-flat. Tensoring $0\\to P^r\\to Q^r\\to H^r\\to0$ with $B_n$ remains exact by [lem-weil-pbc-site-flat-quotient-tensor-ses]; forgetting the $B_n$ action reflects this exactness. Its new cokernel is $H\\otimes_{B_m}B_n$, flat-term K-flat by [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topos-kflat-pullback] for the identity topos and $B_m\\to B_n$. Hence $F_{m,n}(i)$ is a cofibration. If $i$ is trivial, $H$ is acyclic, and $0\\to H$ is a quasi-isomorphism between flat-term K-flat complexes; [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-pullback] says its pullback is a quasi-isomorphism. Thus $F_{m,n}(i)$ is trivial. The chain-level tensor/restriction adjunction of step 1 is therefore a differential graded Quillen adjunction. Its enhanced derived left functor is $T_{m,n}=-\\otimes^L_{B_m}B_n$, with exactly the ordinary computation of [lem-pur-derived-change-of-rings]; its right functor is exact scalar restriction. Apply [lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus] to the associativity isomorphisms $F_{n,r}F_{m,n}\\cong F_{m,r}$ to obtain their derived coherent composition identifications. No $B_n$ is assumed flat as a $B_m$-module.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model",
    "lem-weil-pbc-site-flat-quotient-tensor-ses",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topos-kflat-pullback",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-pullback",
    "lem-pur-derived-change-of-rings",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Take the small category $I=\\mathbf N_{\\ge1}^{\\mathrm{op}}$ and the Quillen diagram just checked. Concretely its free section at $m$ has $(E_m P)_n=P\\otimes_{B_m}B_n$ for $n\\le m$, and zero otherwise; a map from it to a section is uniquely determined by its component $P\\to M_m$. For $n\\le m$, that component forces the map to be $P\\otimes_{B_m}B_n\\to M_m\\otimes_{B_m}B_n\\to M_n$; the cocycle gives compatibility. This verifies the free/evaluation identification and its variance in this diagram. Apply [lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification]: its projective weak equivalences are precisely componentwise quasi-isomorphisms. Via step 1 the source of its enhanced comparison is the required localization of $\\operatorname{Ch}(\\mathscr S(B))$; via step 2 its target is the diagram of enhanced $B_n$-module categories with forward transitions $T_{m,n}$. It is an equivalence over every evaluation, retaining mapping complexes. Its ordinary homotopy category is $D(\\mathscr S(B))$, since the cohomology of a complex of systems is computed componentwise, and both ordinary localizations invert exactly those maps. This uses the derived-category definition, rather than identifying $D(\\mathscr S(B))$ with ordinary sections of the triangulated $D(B_n)$.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification",
    "def-weil-abstract-derived-category"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For a strict complex $P$, choose a projective cofibrant section replacement $Q P\\to P$ as in step 3. Its evaluations are cofibrant and the maps $Q P_n\\to P_n$ are quasi-isomorphisms. Thus the coherent structure arrow is represented by $F_{m,n}(Q P_m)\\to Q P_n$; after the evaluation equivalences it is the composite $T_{m,n}P_m\\simeq F_{m,n}(Q P_m)\\to Q P_n\\to P_n$. Compatibility of $Q P\\to P$ with restriction-form transitions, followed by the adjunction formula of step 1, identifies this composite with the derived adjoint of $P_m\\to\\operatorname{Res}_{m,n}P_n$. Consequently it is cartesian exactly when all the displayed adjoints are equivalences, which is the normalization condition in this statement. For adjacent indices the condition implies it for all $m\\ge n$, because $T_{m,r}\\simeq T_{n,r}T_{m,n}$ and a functor preserves equivalences; the converse includes the adjacent indices. Equivalence detection is the componentwise detection assertion of [lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification]. The flat models and that theorem are unbounded, so nothing in these conclusions used a boundedness or coefficient-flatness assumption.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus"
   ],
   "elementary": true,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Write $V_n=u^{-1}$ on $B_n$-modules with target $C_n=u^{-1}B_n$ and $G_n=Ru_{*,n}$. Module inverse image is exact: finite limits come from the topos inverse image and colimits from its being a left adjoint, with the induced module action. For a flat-model cofibration its cokernel remains flat-term K-flat by [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topos-kflat-pullback]; exactness preserves the monomorphism and the acyclic-cokernel condition. Thus $V_n\\dashv u_{*,n}$ is differential graded Quillen by [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-module-adjunction], and [lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus] gives $V_n\\dashv G_n$ with enhanced units $\\eta_n$ and counits $\\epsilon_n$. Denote the coefficient extension on the source site by $T_{m,n}$ and on the inverse-image site by $S_{m,n}$. The canonical tensor comparison gives a coherent equivalence $q_{m,n}:S_{m,n}V_m\\simeq V_nT_{m,n}$: on flat representatives it is the ordinary inverse-image tensor isomorphism, and [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-tensor-pullback] and the coherent calculus identify its derived version. Set $(VM)_n=V_nM_n$, with transition $S_{m,n}V_mM_m\\xrightarrow{q}V_nT_{m,n}M_m\\xrightarrow{V_n\\tau^M}V_nM_n$. This is a coherent section by naturality and the tensor associativity diagrams; every higher compatibility is the image of those diagrams under the enhanced localization calculus.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topos-kflat-pullback",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-module-adjunction",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-tensor-pullback",
    "lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Define the enhanced mate $b_{m,n}:T_{m,n}G_m\\to G_nS_{m,n}$ to be the transpose under $V_n\\dashv G_n$ of\n$$V_nT_{m,n}G_m\\xrightarrow{q^{-1}}S_{m,n}V_mG_m\\xrightarrow{S_{m,n}\\epsilon_m}S_{m,n}.$$\nFor a coherent section $N$ set $(GN)_n=G_nN_n$ and set its transition to $G_n(\\tau^N_{m,n})\\,b_{m,n,N_m}$. The mate formula in [def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate], applied with $L_1=V_m,L_2=V_n,G=T_{m,n},H=S_{m,n},\\alpha=q^{-1}$, fixes the direction. For a composable pair, the iterated mate is the mate of the pasted inverse $q$'s, by [lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus]; hence applying $G_r$ to the cocycle of $N$ supplies the cocycle of $GN$, including its higher homotopies. The component mapping adjunction sends $h_n:V_nM_n\\to N_n$ to $G_n(h_n)\\eta_{n,M_n}$. Explicitly, the section condition for $h$ is\n$$h_n V_n(\\tau^M_{m,n})=\\tau^N_{m,n}S_{m,n}(h_m)q^{-1}_{m,n,M_m}.$$\nTransposing the left side gives $g_n\\tau^M_{m,n}$, where $g_n=G_n(h_n)\\eta_{n,M_n}$. Naturality of $q$, the defining transpose of $b$, and $\\epsilon_m V_m(\\eta_m)=1$ identify the transpose of the right side with $G_n(\\tau^N_{m,n})b_{m,n,N_m}T_{m,n}(g_m)$. These are exactly the two section composites for $g$. The enriched mate calculus makes this an equivalence of compatibility homotopies for each string of arrows. Thus these equivalences identify the coherent mapping ends of [lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification]. They give $V\\dashv G$ on sections, with inverse transpose $g_n\\mapsto\\epsilon_{n,N_n}V_n(g_n)$. The enhanced triangle identities give actual coherent section units and counits. Transport these functors and maps through step 3. Their derived evaluations are $u^{-1}$ and $Ru_{*,n}$ by construction. This construction uses no claim that evaluation sends a K-injective system to a K-injective complex, and does not require $b_{m,n}$ to be invertible.",
   "uses": [
    "def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate",
    "lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For an open localization $j$ use the exact adjunction $j_!\\dashv j^*$ of [lem-pur-site-restriction-module-adjunction-exact]. For a flat module $F$ on the slice, tensoring a short exact sequence on the ambient site with $j_!F$ is, by [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-slice-extension-tensor], the result of applying exact $j_!$ to its exact restriction tensored with $F$. Thus $j_!F$ is flat. The same projection identity shows that $j_!P$ is K-flat whenever $P$ is: for an acyclic test complex $A$, $(j_!P)\\otimes A\\cong j_!(P\\otimes j^*A)$ is acyclic, since $j^*$ and $j_!$ are exact. Applied to cokernels this checks that $j_!$ preserves cofibrations and trivial cofibrations of the flat model. The same projection identity, taking $A=B_n[0]$, gives the coefficient comparison $T_{m,n}j_!\\simeq j_!S_{m,n}$. On representatives the map carries $x\\otimes b$ to $x\\otimes j^*b$ on the corresponding extension summand; composing coefficient maps gives the same balanced tensor, so these maps satisfy the cocycle. The enhanced calculus gives the coherent derived adjunction and comparisons. Use these coefficient comparisons and the transition formula of step 5, with $j_!$ in place of $V$, to obtain extension by zero on coherent sections, and then step 3 gives its strict derived-system model.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-pur-site-restriction-module-adjunction-exact",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-slice-extension-tensor",
    "lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For a compatible map of ring systems, apply the coefficient extension Quillen adjunction of step 2 at every index. Rebracketing $(P\\otimes_{B_m}B_n)\\otimes_{B_n}D_n$ as $P\\otimes_{B_m}D_n$ and comparing with $(P\\otimes_{B_m}D_m)\\otimes_{D_m}D_n$ gives the coefficient compatibility; here $D_m\\to D_n$ denotes the target system, not a derived category. The enhanced calculus makes these into coherent comparisons. For tensor of two sections set $(M\\otimes^L N)_n=M_n\\otimes^L_{B_n}N_n$; its transition is\n$$T_{m,n}(M_m\\otimes^L_{B_m}N_m)\\simeq T_{m,n}M_m\\otimes^L_{B_n}T_{m,n}N_m\\xrightarrow{\\tau^M\\otimes\\tau^N}M_n\\otimes^L_{B_n}N_n.$$\nThe first arrow is the strong monoidal coefficient extension comparison, coming from balanced tensor rebracketing on cofibrant representatives. Associativity, units and symmetry for these transitions are supplied by the monoidal clause of [lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus]. Cofibrant section replacements have K-flat flat-term evaluations by [lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification] and [lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model], so these evaluated operations are computed by the total tensor complex of [def-pc-tensor-complex-sheaves]. For fiberwise internal Hom take a cartesian first input $M$ and any section $N$. Put $H_n=R\\mathcal Hom_{B_n}(M_n,N_n)$ [def-pur-derived-internal-hom]. To identify this with the internal Hom of the flat model, choose a termwise injective K-injective resolution $N_n\\to I$ [prop-pur-kinjective-resolutions-exist]. Such $I$ is fibrant in the flat model: for a trivial cofibration $A\\hookrightarrow B$ with acyclic cokernel $C$, extend a map $A\\to I$ to a graded map $\\widetilde f:B\\to I$ by termwise injectivity. Its differential defect vanishes on $A$, so factors as a closed degree-one map $z:C\\to I$. K-injectivity applied to the appropriate shift of acyclic $C$ says $z=d(k)$ for a degree-zero graded map $k:C\\to I$ [def-pur-kinjective-complex]. Subtracting $k$ composed with $B\\to C$ from $\\widetilde f$ gives a chain-map extension. This is the required lifting property. If $P\\to M_n$ is a cofibrant replacement, the model internal Hom is therefore represented by $\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(P,I)$. Precomposition identifies it with $\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(M_n,I)$ in the derived category by [lem-pur-rhom-well-defined], using the identity quasi-isomorphism on $I$. This is precisely the cited K-injective definition of $H_n$, and naturality of that comparison identifies evaluations as well. Write $T=T_{m,n}$ and $\\alpha:TM_m\\simeq M_n$ for the cartesian transition. Define the transition $TH_m\\to H_n$ by tensor-Hom transposition of\n$$TH_m\\otimes^L M_n\\xrightarrow{1\\otimes\\alpha^{-1}}TH_m\\otimes^L TM_m\\simeq T(H_m\\otimes^L M_m)\\xrightarrow{T\\mathrm{ev}}TN_m\\xrightarrow{\\tau^N}N_n.$$\nThis is available in the enhanced closed model calculus. For $m\\ge n\\ge r$, write $h_{m,n}:T_{m,n}H_m\\to H_n$ for this transpose and $\\nu_{m,n}=\\tau^N_{m,n}$. Its defining equality is\n$$\\mathrm{ev}_n(h_{m,n}\\otimes\\alpha_{m,n})=\\nu_{m,n}T_{m,n}(\\mathrm{ev}_m),$$\nusing the strong monoidal rebracketing. Applying it first to $n\\to r$ and then to $m\\to n$ gives\n$$\\mathrm{ev}_r\\bigl((h_{n,r}T_{n,r}h_{m,n})\\otimes(\\alpha_{n,r}T_{n,r}\\alpha_{m,n})\\bigr)=(\\nu_{n,r}T_{n,r}\\nu_{m,n})T_{m,r}(\\mathrm{ev}_m).$$\nThe cocycles identify the second tensor factor with $\\alpha_{m,r}$ and the right side with $\\nu_{m,r}T_{m,r}(\\mathrm{ev}_m)$. Since $\\alpha_{m,r}$ is invertible, enhanced tensor-Hom transposition identifies $h_{n,r}T_{n,r}h_{m,n}$ with $h_{m,r}$. Their higher coherent versions, and naturality in $M,N$, follow from the same enhanced tensor-Hom transposition. By this definition the fiberwise evaluations $H_n\\otimes^L M_n\\to N_n$ are a coherent section map. For a section map $P\\otimes^L M\\to N$, transposition gives $P_n\\to H_n$; the displayed formula converts its transition-compatibility into that of the given evaluation map, and the inverse construction is evaluation. These are inverse on the coherent mapping ends of step 3. Thus evaluation and transposition are constructed on sections for precisely the cartesian-first-input scope stated above. Module-sheaf maps induce their chain maps and then their enhanced localization maps. This proves the simultaneous models for coefficient extension, tensor and the specified evaluations; it does not introduce arbitrary lifts from the homotopy categories.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-sheaf-flat-model",
    "lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus",
    "def-pc-tensor-complex-sheaves",
    "def-pur-derived-internal-hom",
    "prop-pur-kinjective-resolutions-exist",
    "def-pur-kinjective-complex",
    "lem-pur-rhom-well-defined"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For any specified exchange square form its map as the unit/composition/counit word in [def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate], using the enhanced inverse-image composition identifications. Those identifications are induced by composition of the actual inverse-image functors and, for coefficient pullback, the strong monoidal maps of step 8. Each letter has been constructed on coherent sections in steps 5-8, so the same word is a coherent natural transformation there. For an open-extension base-change map use the transpose of its inverse-image/open-restriction square under the enhanced $j_!\\dashv j^*$ instead; this supplies that specified map with all its coherence, without a claim of invertibility. For composites of direct images take the right-adjoint composition comparison supplied by the enhanced calculus, the mate of inverse-image composition; its ordinary evaluated comparison is the one of [lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-pushforward-composition]. This applies in particular to proper morphisms without imposing extra assumptions on the input. Pasting these words gives their composites with open extension and proper composition. The enhanced calculus sends the associativity/naturality diagrams for these words to coherent diagrams. Transporting the entire diagram through the equivalence of step 3 gives simultaneous models, including its maps and homotopies, in the enhanced localization of strict systems.",
   "uses": [
    "def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate",
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus",
    "lem-coef-quillen-section-rectification",
    "lem-weil-pbc-ringed-topoi-derived-pushforward-composition"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-prop-sf-biduality-general/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraphs 1-2: coherent finite operations and enriched adjunction before inverse limits; agent expansion with the explicitly isolated model/localization foundations."
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "In particular the coefficient projection $T_{m,n}G_mN\\to G_nS_{m,n}N$ is exactly $b_{m,n,N}$ of step 6: its transpose is $S_{m,n}(\\epsilon_{m,N})\\,q^{-1}_{m,n,G_mN}$. For a general tensor factor $P$ the same formula reads\n$$u^{-1}(Ru_*N\\otimes^L P)\\simeq u^{-1}Ru_*N\\otimes^L u^{-1}P\\xrightarrow{\\epsilon_N\\otimes1}N\\otimes^L u^{-1}P.$$\nTaking its enhanced adjoint defines the projection comparison, using step 8 for tensor. It and its composites with $j_!$ are therefore the derived counit transposes in these models. An evaluation functor applies to every letter of these displayed words, hence gives the prescribed levelwise projection or exchange formula. Applying evaluation to the unit/composition/counit word for any transpose gives the same word formed after evaluation. The coherent functoriality and triangle homotopies of [lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus] establish this before taking ordinary homotopy categories; no faithfulness of evaluation on morphisms is inferred from its detection of equivalences. This proves the final preservation clause for evaluation, composition and adjunction transposes.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-dg-derived-quillen-calculus",
    "def-weil-pbc-adjunction-mate"
   ],
